On Nov 2, 2011, at 12:42 PM, Altermatt, Bill wrote:

> Thanks, Simon.  I should clarify that my server is UNIX and I'm using it as a remote drive.  R is not installed on that server.  I installed R on a local Windows machine and then copied all of the files from the installation onto the server.  If I create a shortcut to the Rgui.exe file on the server from a local machine, R pops up on the local machine and runs fine.  I also copied the jgr.exe file to the server.  When I make a shortcut to that file from a local machine and double-click it, it either runs the local copy of R (if one is installed) or produces an error message about missing a .dll file (which sounds to me like a registry / installation problem).  Is there a way, perhaps by adding some switches to jgr.exe, to get it to identify the remote copy of R as the one to use?  Or is 
 my whole remote-drive approach doomed to failure and a different approach would be recommended?
> 

JGR gets the information about Java and R from the registry - that is the only way to locate things on Windows as there are no "standard" paths. So all you need to do is to make sure your remote R is registered on the local machine: in SOFTWARE/R-core/R there must be InstallPath entry with the path to R - check the machine that you used to install it for details.

Cheers,
Simon




